// Producer/consumer binding vocabulary.
//
// A Binding maps a declaration-defined key to a concrete value.
// The producer (type declaration) defines the key schema — what
// parameter names exist and in what order. The consumer (type
// instantiation) provides concrete values for each key.
//
// This is the pattern behind:
//   - Type parameters: List<T> instantiated as List<Int> → {T: Int}
//   - Function arguments: fn(x: Int, y: String) called with (1, "hello")
//   - Algebra templates: method with T/K/V resolved to concrete types
//
// The key comes from the declaration (e.g. container_type_param_names
// in std/types.dag). The value is the concrete type/expression at the
// usage site. List<Binding> preserves declaration order for positional
// access while supporting key-based lookup.
//
// This module declares the abstract concept. Compiler-specific bindings
// (where values are Node) are defined in the compiler layer (v1.std.core).

module std.binding

type Binding {
  key: String
  value: String
}
